WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.590
My name is Matthieu Palombino and I am the
chef of Motorino.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.660
A calzone is a folded pizza that is stuffed.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:19.340
What makes a good calzone, is a calzone that
is cheesy and not too wet or saucy.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.980
What a calzone allows you to do is keep the
stuffing moist.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.340
If the ingredients were on top of the pizza,
they would dry out very quickly, and it wouldn't

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.690
be the same.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.750
A friend of mine told me his grandfather's
calzone recipe.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.309
The recipe was well put together, with a nice
stuffing that was a little bit different.

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:39.190
I thought it was just perfect.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.650
So I said, "OK, let's put it on the menu and
see how people like it."

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:47.820
Rocco is his name, so I called it "Rocky's
Calzone," which has nothing to do with Rocky

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:48.820
Balboa.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.200
No, it's a guy from Brooklyn.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.860
The way we do the calzone here at Motorino
is first we make the filling.

00:00:56.869 --> 00:01:02.309
Sweet sausage and spicy sausage both get rendered
in a pot.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:06.470
Once we have the sausage fully cooked we drain
the fat out.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:12.780
We return the crumbled, cooked sausage into
the pot and then we'll add eggs.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.960
Then we'll cook it on medium heat until the
eggs are scrambled, so essentially what you

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.330
have is eggs and sausage scramble.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.790
That's when you add a good amount of Pecorino,
which is has the highest flavor note, and

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.220
some parsley for the color.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.140
Stir it in and that's your filling.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.420
We use the same dough as the pizza.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:35.600
The most important thing is timing: how you
time your fermentation, how you time your

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.600
resting.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.570
You open the calzone the same way you open
a pizza.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.600
Then you place the stuffing on one side.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.790
Then you fold the other side on top.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.620
You seal the edge with your fingers.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.250
Then you put a little bit of garlic, more
Pecorino, and olive oil—just before it goes

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.330
into the oven just to give it a crispier top.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.940
Then you put a little bit of garlic, more
Pecorino, and olive oil—just before it goes

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.630
into the oven just to give it a crispier top.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.600
We use a regular Neapolitan oven, so wood-fired.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.360
And that's it.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.140
That's your calzone.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:17.680
I grew up in Belgium and I ate a lot of Italian
food, especially growing up in an Italian

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.680
family.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.880
I thought that pizza was something that was
perfect for me.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:28.310
I knew that a pizzeria done right had a good
chance to land successfully pretty much anywhere.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.700
In Italy, you go to the pizzeria that you
like and you go over and over because it works

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.700
for you.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.700
That's what my aim is.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.819
I don't aim to be the best in the world.

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:40.829
I just aim to be a good pizzeria that works.

